course: dictionary
lesson: 15048
title: Taking it back
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A retraction is when you take back something you said or printed that was wrong. The word comes from retract, which means to pull back. «The newspaper printed a retraction after it got the story wrong.» The paper pulled its mistake back and told readers the truth.
explain: Retraction is also a science word. It means pulling something back in or out of sight. «The cat pulled its claws in, so they stayed sharp.» The claws went back in, out of sight, and stayed sharp for later.
explain: Look for clues in the words around it. Words like wrong, mistake, or false show that a retraction is about correcting something. Words like claws, wheels, or pulled back in show the science meaning. Check by putting your meaning into the sentence and seeing if it still makes sense.
fact: Retraction means taking back a statement that was wrong.
fact: In science, retraction means pulling something back in or out of sight.
fact: A newspaper may print a retraction when it has got a story wrong.
fact: Cats have retractable claws, which they pull in when they do not need them.
fact: In science, a paper with mistakes may get a retraction, so honest checking matters.
exercise: «The newspaper printed a retraction after it got the story wrong.» What is a retraction here? | *A statement taking back a wrong story | A big party for readers | A picture of a cat | A new photo of the town
exercise: «The cat pulled its claws in, so they stayed sharp.» What does retraction mean in this science sense? | *Pulling something back in or out of sight | Sharpening the claws with a knife | Painting the claws bright red | Growing new claws
exercise: Which sentence shows a retraction? | *A news website admits its report was false and takes it back. | The website adds a new picture of the sea. | The website is shared by a friend. | The website is painted blue.
test: «The scientist sent a retraction after finding an error in her paper.» What does retraction mean? | A prize for good work | *A statement taking back a claim that was wrong | A long holiday | A new experiment
test: «The cat's claws are retractable, so they are safe when she walks indoors.» What does retractable suggest? | Cannot be seen ever | Painted in bright colours | *Can be pulled back in | Made of wood
exam: «The newspaper's retraction said the photo was not from the storm.» Why did the newspaper print a retraction? | It forgot the date | *It had printed something wrong and took it back | It found a new photo | It wanted to sell more papers
